// Compile Error: an inline `.read()` with nothing chained onto it
// ERROR: standalone `.read()` on `Shared` has nothing chained onto it
//
// R5 scopes an inline access to the chain it starts, so `cfg.read()` with
// nothing after it takes a lock, releases it, and hands back a value that was
// only valid while it was held.
//
// A bare `let x = cfg.read()` was already rejected; an *operand* wasn't (#958),
// because desugaring turns `cfg.read() + 1` into `cfg.read().add(1)` and that
// looked like a chain. Native then dispatched to the closure-taking runtime
// entry point with the element size sitting where the closure belongs, built a
// function pointer out of the integer 8 and called it: `Shared.new(41)` printed
// 49 natively and 42 on the interpreter.
//
// The working spellings are in `tests/suite/t_shared_inline_access.rk`.

import sync.Shared

func main() {
    let cfg = Shared.new(41)
    let a = cfg.read() + 1
    println("{a}")
}
